<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Europe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Europe is the western part of the Eurasian landmass. It was the home of empires such as the Roman, Carolingian, Holy Roman and Austro-Hungarian, and from the fifteenth century European states built overseas empires that reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Through trade, conquest and diplomacy, Europe figures in the histories of almost every empire covered by the site, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/europe.jpg" alt="Europe" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob984 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europe_orthographic_Caucasus_Urals_boundary_(with_borders).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Alans were a nomadic Iranian-speaking people whose cavalry dominated the steppe north of the Caucasus, raided Rome and Iran, and after the Hunnic invasions scattered across <b>Europe</b> and Africa, while those who stayed in the Caucasus became the ancestors of the Ossetians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Alemanni changed the map of western <b>Europe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Armenia ratified the Treaty on Conventional Armed Forces in <b>Europe</b> in 1992, acceded to the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty in 1993, joined the Collective Security Treaty Organisation and sent troops to NATO's KFOR in Kosovo in 2004; Russia keeps a base at Gyumri as a deterrent against Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Appeals to <b>Europe</b> and plans for new crusades brought nothing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna, held in its capital in 1814–1815, redrew the map of <b>Europe</b> and established the system of great-power diplomacy known as the Concert of Europe, and Metternich's name became shorthand for conservative restoration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Austro-Hungarian economy underwent a sustained transformation across the empire's fifty-one years, moving from a predominantly agrarian base in 1867 towards one of the larger industrial economies of continental <b>Europe</b> by 1914.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
